# Erdős Problem #99 — weighted minority allocation sharpens the atomic train gap to 0.00058

**Date:** 2026-09-14  
**Target:** Target C-II canonical full-core Type-II branch  
**Claim class:** proof-only sharpening of AG47; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**External input:** none beyond the already-used Mantel stability, König theorem, and §163 crossing-order machinery  
**Computation:** exact rational interval certificate for the final constants

## LEVEL I STATUS

`STILL OPEN`

The AG47 chain-cover refinement proves

\[
 x\sigma\ge 2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.00047,
 \qquad
 \Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3}.
\]

Its remaining quantitative loss is not in the chain cover. It occurs one step earlier, when the three dominant-orbit atom windows are assigned to the two Mantel shores.

AG47 first discards every minority copy of each atom window, obtains a lower retained mass `S_0` on **each** shore, and then uses the `1+2` support split to select opposite-label families of masses `S_0` and `S_0/2`. This pays the same worst-case minority loss on both shores.

That is unnecessary. The three labels are assigned to disjoint shores after minority deletion, so the selected `1+2` pair depends on the **total** minority loss, not on the worst loss repeated on both shores. The finite allocation lemma below replaces

\[
 {3\over2}(S-L)
\]

by

\[
 {3S\over2}-L.
\]

This recovers exactly `L/2` in the opposite-corner path and raises the certifiable atomic gap to

\[
\boxed{
 x\sigma\ge2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.00058.
}
\tag{AG58.1}
\]

Thus, at the same proof-only scope,

\[
\boxed{\eta_{grid}=0.00058.}
\tag{AG58.2}
\]

The numerical root of this unchanged bookkeeping is about `0.0005819424`; the stated `0.00058` keeps a positive exact interval margin.

---

## 1. Dominant orbit and finite Mantel partition

Put

\[
 s_2=\sqrt{\Delta_2},
 \qquad
 \eta=0.00058,
 \qquad
 \bar A=2s_2+\eta.
\]

Assume for contradiction that

\[
 A=x\sigma\le\bar A.
\]

Exactly as in audited §168 and AG47, one dominant three-direction projective orbit has mass at least

\[
 M_0={4s_2^2\over\bar A},
\tag{AG58.3}
\]

and the total atomic mass outside it is at most

\[
 R_0=\bar A-M_0.
\tag{AG58.4}
\]

Use the same conservative dominant-orbit Mantel deficit

\[
 d_0={\bar A^2\over4}-s_2^2+{R_0^2\over4}.
\tag{AG58.5}
\]

Freezing small continuity windows around the at most three dominant-orbit atoms and taking a sufficiently far finite source gives a triangle-free train-crossing graph. The maximum-degree Mantel partition `X\sqcup Y` satisfies

\[
 |X|,|Y|
 \ge
 \left({M_0\over2}-\sqrt{d_0}-o(1)\right)D,
\tag{AG58.6}
\]

and at most

\[
 (2d_0+o(1))D^2
\tag{AG58.7}
\]

cross-shore pairs are missing.

Define the pre-assignment shore floor

\[
\boxed{
 S:={M_0\over2}-\sqrt{d_0}.
}
\tag{AG58.8}
\]

For the three atom windows let `x_i,y_i` be their normalized populations on `X,Y`. Same-window pairs cannot be strict-rhombus crossings, hence

\[
 \sum_{i=0}^2x_i y_i\le2d_0+o(1).
\tag{AG58.9}
\]

Put

\[
 \ell_i:=\min(x_i,y_i),
 \qquad
 L:=\sum_i\ell_i.
\]

Then Cauchy gives

\[
\boxed{
 L\le\sqrt{3\sum_i x_i y_i}
 \le\sqrt{6d_0}+o(1).
}
\tag{AG58.10}
\]

---

## 2. Weighted minority-allocation lemma

### Lemma AG58.1

Let three labels be distributed between two shores with total shore masses at least `S`, and let

\[
 L=\sum_i\min(x_i,y_i).
\]

Assign each label to a shore on which it has weakly larger mass and delete its population on the other shore. If both retained shores are nonempty, then there are distinct labels `i\ne j`, one retained on each shore, such that

\[
\boxed{
 x_i+y_j\ge {3S\over2}-L,
}
\tag{AG58.11}
\]

and individually

\[
\boxed{
 x_i,y_j\ge {S-L\over2}.
}
\tag{AG58.12}
\]

### Proof

Write the deleted minority masses from the two shores as `L_X,L_Y`, so

\[
 L_X+L_Y=L.
\]

The retained shore totals are at least

\[
 S-L_X,
 \qquad
 S-L_Y.
\]

After assignment the label supports of the two shores are disjoint. Since there are only three labels and both supports are nonempty, their support sizes are one of

\[
 (1,1),\quad(1,2),\quad(2,1).
\]

If the support sizes are `(1,2)`, the unique label on the first shore has mass at least `S-L_X`, while the larger label on the second has mass at least `(S-L_Y)/2`. Their sum is at least

\[
 S-L_X+{S-L_Y\over2}
 ={3S\over2}-L+{L_Y\over2}
 \ge {3S\over2}-L.
\]

The `(2,1)` case is symmetric. In the `(1,1)` case the sum is at least

\[
 2S-L\ge{3S\over2}-L.
\]

Every selected label is at least `(S-L)/2`: a singleton retained shore has mass at least `S-L`, while the larger label on a two-label retained shore has at least half of that shore's mass. This proves both claims. `\square`

### Application

Here `S-L>0` by the numerical certificate below, so both retained shores are nonempty. Therefore there are opposite-shore atom families `A_D,B_D`, with distinct projective labels and axes separated by `pi/3`, satisfying

\[
\boxed{
 {|A_D|+|B_D|\over D}
 \ge {3S\over2}-\sqrt{6d_0}-o(1),
}
\tag{AG58.13}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
 {|A_D|\over D}, {|B_D|\over D}
 \ge {S-\sqrt{6d_0}\over2}-o(1).
}
\tag{AG58.14}
\]

Their total missing-pair count is still bounded by (AG58.7).

The improvement over AG47 is exact: AG47 used the pair-sum floor

\[
 {3\over2}(S-\sqrt{6d_0}),
\]

while (AG58.13) uses

\[
 {3S\over2}-\sqrt{6d_0}.
\]

The recovered amount is

\[
 {1\over2}\sqrt{6d_0}.
\tag{AG58.15}
\]

---

## 3. Reuse the AG47 chain cover

Choose the same projective lifts as AG47,

\[
 x_u=\alpha_u,
 \qquad
 y_v=\beta_v-\pi/3.
\]

Every inversion `x_u\ge y_v` is a missing pair. The inversion graph is a chain graph, and it has at most `(2d_0+o(1))D^2` edges.

Let

\[
 p_0:=\sqrt{2d_0}.
\tag{AG58.16}
\]

The sparse-chain vertex-cover lemma therefore removes all inversions by deleting in total at most

\[
 (2\sqrt{d_0}+o(1))D
 = (\sqrt2\,p_0+o(1))D
\tag{AG58.17}
\]

trains across the two shores.

After phase ordering, delete from each shore every train missing more than `(p_0+o(1))D` trains on the opposite shore. Since the total missing count is at most `p_0^2D^2+o(D^2)`, this deletes at most `(p_0+o(1))D` trains from each shore. Every remaining train then misses at most `(p_0+o(1))D` opposite trains.

The worst possible individual-family size after chain-cover and degree pruning is at least

\[
 {S-\sqrt{6d_0}\over2}
 -(\sqrt2+1)p_0-o(1).
\tag{AG58.18}
\]

Thus every triple on the opposite shore has a common neighbor whenever

\[
 {S-\sqrt{6d_0}\over2}>(4+\sqrt2)p_0.
\tag{AG58.19}
\]

The exact certificate below gives a margin greater than `0.2554`, so the §163 actual-`K_{2,2}` order-consistency and triple-neighbor transitivity argument applies without modification.

---

## 4. Opposite-corner contradiction

Before phase and degree pruning the selected pair has total normalized mass at least

\[
 {3S\over2}-\sqrt{6d_0}.
\]

The chain cover costs at most `sqrt(2)p_0` in total, degree pruning costs at most `2p_0` in total, and the two opposite-corner endpoint trains together miss at most another `2p_0` opposite trains.

Hence the selected degree sum in the §163 two-leg path is at least

\[
\boxed{
 {3S\over2}-\sqrt{6d_0}-(4+\sqrt2)p_0-o(1).
}
\tag{AG58.20}
\]

The atom windows shrink to axes separated by `pi/3`, so every selected elementary train displacement has common projection tending uniformly to `sqrt(3)/2`. Therefore the source diameter gives

\[
1\ge {\sqrt3\over2}
\left[
 {3S\over2}-\sqrt{6d_0}-(4+\sqrt2)p_0
\right]-o(1).
\tag{AG58.21}
\]

For `eta=0.00058`, exact interval arithmetic gives

\[
\begin{aligned}
M_0&=1.904045790296783\ldots,\\
R_0&=0.001159823431131\ldots,\\
d_0&=0.000552761825579\ldots,\\
S&=0.928512007764479\ldots,\\
\sqrt{6d_0}&=0.057589677490625\ldots,\\
p_0&=0.033249415801756\ldots .
\end{aligned}
\tag{AG58.22}
\]

The projected lower bound is

\[
\boxed{
 {\sqrt3\over2}
\left[
 {3S\over2}-\sqrt{6d_0}-(4+\sqrt2)p_0
\right]
>1.0003969.
}
\tag{AG58.23}
\]

This contradicts (AG58.21), proving (AG58.1).

---

## 5. Scope and research effect

- The proof uses the same dominant-orbit reduction and finite Mantel partition as audited §168 and AG47.
- The only new theorem is the finite three-label allocation Lemma AG58.1.
- The AG47 chain-graph phase cover, degree pruning, `K_{2,2}` order consistency, and opposite-corner diameter path are reused unchanged.
- No new external theorem, reverse Oler claim, source deletion, or packing competitor is introduced.
- Contact-isolated source points remain in the full source.
- The constant `0.00058` is proof-only until independently audited and promoted.

This improvement does not close C-II. It does show that the current quantitative atomic-train mechanism had additional slack in a finite allocation step, not in the geometric opposite-corner argument.

The exact certificate is `research/verify_target_c_atomic_gap_weighted_minority_00058.py`.

**Disposition:** proved proof-only candidate. C-II and Level I remain open.
